1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,066
Sweetheart, sit here by me.

2
00:00:02,246 --> 00:00:04,674
You asked me what year I would

3
00:00:04,674 --> 00:00:05,565
go back to,

4
00:00:05,565 --> 00:00:07,669
if I could go back to just

5
00:00:07,669 --> 00:00:07,993
one.

6
00:00:08,173 --> 00:00:10,321
Well, I've thought about it.

7
00:00:10,501 --> 00:00:12,614
It's nineteen sixty-four.

8
00:00:12,794 --> 00:00:14,408
I was twenty years old.

9
00:00:14,588 --> 00:00:16,840
I'd been married for six months.

10
00:00:17,020 --> 00:00:19,469
And the whole country felt like it

11
00:00:19,469 --> 00:00:21,630
was waking up from a bad dream

12
00:00:21,630 --> 00:00:23,719
and dancing at the same time.

13
00:00:24,068 --> 00:00:26,586
You have to understand how that year

14
00:00:26,586 --> 00:00:27,006
began.

15
00:00:27,186 --> 00:00:28,710
Two months before,

16
00:00:28,710 --> 00:00:30,996
in November of sixty-three,

17
00:00:30,996 --> 00:00:34,129
President Kennedy was shot in Dallas.

18
00:00:34,309 --> 00:00:36,694
I was at the Woolworth's counter when

19
00:00:36,694 --> 00:00:37,855
the radio said it,

20
00:00:37,855 --> 00:00:40,305
and every person in that store stopped

21
00:00:40,305 --> 00:00:40,949
and cried.

22
00:00:41,129 --> 00:00:42,314
Grown men.

23
00:00:42,494 --> 00:00:45,049
The whole country was in mourning that

24
00:00:45,049 --> 00:00:45,721
Christmas.

25
00:00:45,901 --> 00:00:48,804
Nobody felt like celebrating anything.

26
00:00:49,153 --> 00:00:51,492
Your grandfather and I lived in a

27
00:00:51,492 --> 00:00:54,398
little apartment over a hardware store on

28
00:00:54,398 --> 00:00:56,595
North Main Street in Mansfield.

29
00:00:56,775 --> 00:00:59,207
Two rooms and a kitchen the size

30
00:00:59,207 --> 00:01:00,119
of a closet.

31
00:01:00,299 --> 00:01:03,273
The rent was seventy-five dollars a month,

32
00:01:03,273 --> 00:01:05,326
and that felt like a fortune.

33
00:01:05,506 --> 00:01:07,788
The radiator banged all night,

34
00:01:07,788 --> 00:01:10,374
and the man downstairs sold paint,

35
00:01:10,374 --> 00:01:13,645
so the whole place smelled like turpentine.

36
00:01:13,825 --> 00:01:15,764
I thought it was a palace.

37
00:01:16,113 --> 00:01:19,732
Your grandfather worked at the Westinghouse plant,

38
00:01:19,732 --> 00:01:20,817
like my father,

39
00:01:20,817 --> 00:01:23,567
on the line that made electric ranges.

40
00:01:23,747 --> 00:01:26,119
He made about two dollars and forty

41
00:01:26,119 --> 00:01:27,068
cents an hour.

42
00:01:27,248 --> 00:01:29,818
I worked at the bank downtown as

43
00:01:29,818 --> 00:01:32,389
a teller, until I was expecting,

44
00:01:32,389 --> 00:01:34,156
because in those days,

45
00:01:34,156 --> 00:01:36,968
sweetheart, a lot of places let you

46
00:01:36,968 --> 00:01:39,217
go when you started to show.

47
00:01:39,397 --> 00:01:41,928
Nobody thought that was strange.

48
00:01:42,108 --> 00:01:43,896
It was just the rule.

49
00:01:44,246 --> 00:01:45,357
We had one car,

50
00:01:45,357 --> 00:01:48,984
a nineteen fifty-seven Ford that your grandfather

51
00:01:48,984 --> 00:01:51,723
bought used for four hundred dollars.

52
00:01:51,903 --> 00:01:54,655
Gas was about thirty cents a gallon.

53
00:01:54,835 --> 00:01:57,395
On Sunday afternoons we'd fill it up

54
00:01:57,395 --> 00:01:59,742
for three dollars and just drive,

55
00:01:59,742 --> 00:02:02,160
out past the farms to Malabar Farm

56
00:02:02,160 --> 00:02:04,650
and back, with the windows down and

57
00:02:04,650 --> 00:02:05,574
the radio on.

58
00:02:05,754 --> 00:02:07,020
That was a date.

59
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:09,301
It didn't cost anything.

60
00:02:09,650 --> 00:02:12,390
And then came the ninth of February.

61
00:02:12,570 --> 00:02:13,697
A Sunday.

62
00:02:13,877 --> 00:02:15,665
I will never forget it.

63
00:02:15,845 --> 00:02:18,394
The Beatles were on The Ed Sullivan

64
00:02:18,394 --> 00:02:18,759
Show.

65
00:02:18,939 --> 00:02:21,091
We didn't have a television yet,

66
00:02:21,091 --> 00:02:23,175
so we went to my parents' house

67
00:02:23,175 --> 00:02:24,117
on Elm Street.

68
00:02:24,297 --> 00:02:26,487
My brother Danny was sixteen,

69
00:02:26,487 --> 00:02:28,828
and he had combed his hair down

70
00:02:28,828 --> 00:02:31,472
over his forehead for the occasion.

71
00:02:31,652 --> 00:02:33,928
My father thought he looked like a

72
00:02:33,928 --> 00:02:34,531
sheepdog.

73
00:02:34,880 --> 00:02:38,224
Seventy-three million people watched that night,

74
00:02:38,224 --> 00:02:38,642
honey.

75
00:02:38,822 --> 00:02:40,877
Almost half the country.

76
00:02:41,057 --> 00:02:43,682
When those four boys started playing I

77
00:02:43,682 --> 00:02:45,271
Want to Hold Your Hand,

78
00:02:45,271 --> 00:02:48,104
the girls in the studio audience screamed

79
00:02:48,104 --> 00:02:50,383
so loud you could barely hear the

80
00:02:50,383 --> 00:02:50,798
music.

81
00:02:50,978 --> 00:02:53,172
Danny was jumping up and down on

82
00:02:53,172 --> 00:02:53,857
the couch.

83
00:02:54,037 --> 00:02:55,232
And my father,

84
00:02:55,232 --> 00:02:57,279
who never said anything,

85
00:02:57,279 --> 00:02:59,668
put down his paper and said,

86
00:02:59,668 --> 00:03:01,375
well, they can sing.

87
00:03:01,555 --> 00:03:03,029
I'll give them that.

88
00:03:03,380 --> 00:03:05,636
By April the Beatles had the top

89
00:03:05,636 --> 00:03:08,035
five songs in the whole country at

90
00:03:08,035 --> 00:03:09,022
the same time.

91
00:03:09,202 --> 00:03:12,000
Nobody had ever done that before.

92
00:03:12,180 --> 00:03:15,640
Every radio station played them every hour.

93
00:03:15,820 --> 00:03:19,036
Your grandfather pretended he didn't like them.

94
00:03:19,216 --> 00:03:21,654
Then I caught him singing She Loves

95
00:03:21,654 --> 00:03:22,908
You in the shower.

96
00:03:23,088 --> 00:03:24,887
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

97
00:03:25,067 --> 00:03:26,994
He never lived it down.

98
00:03:27,344 --> 00:03:29,569
Now let me tell you what things

99
00:03:29,569 --> 00:03:32,081
cost, because you won't believe me.

100
00:03:32,261 --> 00:03:35,315
A loaf of bread was twenty-one cents.

101
00:03:35,495 --> 00:03:37,538
A gallon of milk was about a

102
00:03:37,538 --> 00:03:38,049
dollar.

103
00:03:38,229 --> 00:03:41,317
A first-class stamp was five cents.

104
00:03:41,497 --> 00:03:43,808
A movie ticket was a dollar.

105
00:03:43,988 --> 00:03:45,814
A brand new house in a nice

106
00:03:45,814 --> 00:03:48,520
part of town was about eighteen thousand

107
00:03:48,520 --> 00:03:49,061
dollars.

108
00:03:49,241 --> 00:03:51,525
And the minimum wage was a dollar

109
00:03:51,525 --> 00:03:52,910
twenty-five an hour.

110
00:03:53,260 --> 00:03:55,508
In April Ford came out with a

111
00:03:55,508 --> 00:03:57,602
new car called the Mustang.

112
00:03:57,782 --> 00:04:00,649
It was so pretty it stopped traffic.

113
00:04:00,829 --> 00:04:03,189
The base price was two thousand three

114
00:04:03,189 --> 00:04:05,229
hundred and sixty-eight dollars.

115
00:04:05,409 --> 00:04:08,125
They sold twenty-two thousand of them on

116
00:04:08,125 --> 00:04:09,415
the very first day.

117
00:04:09,595 --> 00:04:12,152
Your grandfather stood in front of the

118
00:04:12,152 --> 00:04:15,045
dealership window on Park Avenue for twenty

119
00:04:15,045 --> 00:04:17,467
minutes like a little boy looking at

120
00:04:17,467 --> 00:04:18,140
a bicycle.

121
00:04:18,320 --> 00:04:19,667
We couldn't afford it.

122
00:04:19,847 --> 00:04:22,401
We couldn't afford it for ten years.

123
00:04:22,751 --> 00:04:24,770
We did our shopping at the A

124
00:04:24,770 --> 00:04:26,068
and P on Saturday,

125
00:04:26,068 --> 00:04:28,160
and I had a budget of fifteen

126
00:04:28,160 --> 00:04:29,891
dollars a week for food.

127
00:04:30,071 --> 00:04:31,557
Fifteen dollars.

128
00:04:31,737 --> 00:04:34,621
That bought meat for every night,

129
00:04:34,621 --> 00:04:36,195
a roast on Sunday,

130
00:04:36,195 --> 00:04:38,030
eggs, butter, coffee,

131
00:04:38,030 --> 00:04:40,827
and a box of Jell-O for dessert.

132
00:04:41,007 --> 00:04:43,623
And I clipped every coupon in the

133
00:04:43,623 --> 00:04:45,446
Mansfield News Journal,

134
00:04:45,446 --> 00:04:47,427
and I saved S and H Green

135
00:04:47,427 --> 00:04:49,250
Stamps in little books,

136
00:04:49,250 --> 00:04:51,073
to trade for a toaster.

137
00:04:51,424 --> 00:04:52,649
And at the bank,

138
00:04:52,649 --> 00:04:55,864
sweetheart, I saw something every day that

139
00:04:55,864 --> 00:04:57,472
would make you angry.

140
00:04:57,652 --> 00:04:59,878
A woman could come to my window

141
00:04:59,878 --> 00:05:01,960
and deposit her own paycheck.

142
00:05:02,140 --> 00:05:03,840
But if she wanted a loan,

143
00:05:03,840 --> 00:05:06,358
or a charge account at the department

144
00:05:06,358 --> 00:05:08,943
store, she needed her husband to sign.

145
00:05:09,123 --> 00:05:11,303
Even if she earned more than he

146
00:05:11,303 --> 00:05:11,584
did.

147
00:05:11,764 --> 00:05:13,715
Even a widow had trouble.

148
00:05:13,895 --> 00:05:17,389
That didn't change until nineteen seventy-four.

149
00:05:17,569 --> 00:05:20,161
I'd stamp her papers and keep my

150
00:05:20,161 --> 00:05:21,052
mouth shut.

151
00:05:21,232 --> 00:05:23,009
Everybody did.

152
00:05:23,359 --> 00:05:26,146
That summer the World's Fair opened in

153
00:05:26,146 --> 00:05:26,807
New York.

154
00:05:26,987 --> 00:05:28,995
Everybody wanted to go.

155
00:05:29,175 --> 00:05:31,349
It had a giant steel globe,

156
00:05:31,349 --> 00:05:32,959
twelve stories high,

157
00:05:32,959 --> 00:05:34,650
called the Unisphere,

158
00:05:34,650 --> 00:05:37,710
and pavilions from all over the world,

159
00:05:37,710 --> 00:05:40,286
and things nobody had ever seen.

160
00:05:40,466 --> 00:05:43,015
A telephone where you could see the

161
00:05:43,015 --> 00:05:44,471
other person's face.

162
00:05:44,651 --> 00:05:46,692
A computer, a real one,

163
00:05:46,692 --> 00:05:48,378
the size of a room,

164
00:05:48,378 --> 00:05:50,863
that could answer questions.

165
00:05:51,213 --> 00:05:53,240
We drove there in August,

166
00:05:53,240 --> 00:05:55,186
your grandfather and me,

167
00:05:55,186 --> 00:05:56,565
in that old Ford.

168
00:05:56,745 --> 00:05:58,521
It took two days.

169
00:05:58,701 --> 00:06:01,072
We slept in the car one night

170
00:06:01,072 --> 00:06:02,625
outside Harrisburg.

171
00:06:02,805 --> 00:06:05,465
At the General Motors pavilion they had

172
00:06:05,465 --> 00:06:08,056
a ride called Futurama that showed you

173
00:06:08,056 --> 00:06:09,556
the world of tomorrow,

174
00:06:09,556 --> 00:06:11,874
with cities under the ocean and on

175
00:06:11,874 --> 00:06:12,488
the moon.

176
00:06:12,668 --> 00:06:15,036
We stood in line for two hours.

177
00:06:15,216 --> 00:06:17,279
I thought, by the time I'm old,

178
00:06:17,279 --> 00:06:19,408
we'll all be living on the moon.

179
00:06:19,588 --> 00:06:20,424
Well.

180
00:06:20,604 --> 00:06:21,753
Here I am,

181
00:06:21,753 --> 00:06:23,018
sweetheart.

182
00:06:23,198 --> 00:06:24,476
Still in Ohio.

183
00:06:24,826 --> 00:06:26,950
And Disney had made a boat ride

184
00:06:26,950 --> 00:06:28,527
for the Pepsi pavilion,

185
00:06:28,527 --> 00:06:31,268
with hundreds of little dolls from every

186
00:06:31,268 --> 00:06:33,324
country singing the same song.

187
00:06:33,504 --> 00:06:35,257
It's a Small World.

188
00:06:35,437 --> 00:06:36,575
I cried on it.

189
00:06:36,755 --> 00:06:38,671
I don't know why.

190
00:06:38,851 --> 00:06:41,088
I was twenty years old and I'd

191
00:06:41,088 --> 00:06:43,252
never seen anything but Ohio,

192
00:06:43,252 --> 00:06:46,012
and here were children from the whole

193
00:06:46,012 --> 00:06:47,802
world, singing together.

194
00:06:47,982 --> 00:06:49,584
It just got me.

195
00:06:49,934 --> 00:06:52,884
But it wasn't all music and fairs,

196
00:06:52,884 --> 00:06:53,405
honey.

197
00:06:53,585 --> 00:06:56,354
That summer, on the second of July,

198
00:06:56,354 --> 00:06:59,994
President Johnson signed the Civil Rights Act.

199
00:07:00,174 --> 00:07:02,214
It made it against the law to

200
00:07:02,214 --> 00:07:04,607
keep people out of a restaurant or

201
00:07:04,607 --> 00:07:06,507
a hotel or a job because of

202
00:07:06,507 --> 00:07:08,196
the color of their skin.

203
00:07:08,376 --> 00:07:10,830
You'd be amazed how many people in

204
00:07:10,830 --> 00:07:13,067
our town were angry about that.

205
00:07:13,247 --> 00:07:15,036
Some of them sat in my own

206
00:07:15,036 --> 00:07:16,137
family's church.

207
00:07:16,488 --> 00:07:18,549
And three young men who went to

208
00:07:18,549 --> 00:07:21,607
Mississippi that summer to help Black families

209
00:07:21,607 --> 00:07:23,535
register to vote disappeared.

210
00:07:23,715 --> 00:07:25,627
They were found in August,

211
00:07:25,627 --> 00:07:26,803
buried in a dam.

212
00:07:26,983 --> 00:07:29,412
Two of them were from New York,

213
00:07:29,412 --> 00:07:31,058
one from Mississippi.

214
00:07:31,238 --> 00:07:34,325
I remember your grandfather reading that in

215
00:07:34,325 --> 00:07:36,981
the paper at breakfast and putting it

216
00:07:36,981 --> 00:07:39,063
down and not eating his eggs.

217
00:07:39,243 --> 00:07:42,181
He said, that's somebody's son.

218
00:07:42,361 --> 00:07:43,928
Three somebodies.

219
00:07:44,278 --> 00:07:46,194
And there was a place on the

220
00:07:46,194 --> 00:07:48,863
other side of the world called Vietnam,

221
00:07:48,863 --> 00:07:51,054
that most of us couldn't find on

222
00:07:51,054 --> 00:07:51,464
a map.

223
00:07:51,644 --> 00:07:54,074
In August the president said our ships

224
00:07:54,074 --> 00:07:56,312
had been attacked in a place called

225
00:07:56,312 --> 00:07:57,527
the Gulf of Tonkin,

226
00:07:57,527 --> 00:07:59,701
and Congress gave him the power to

227
00:07:59,701 --> 00:08:00,085
fight.

228
00:08:00,265 --> 00:08:02,522
It was a little story on page

229
00:08:02,522 --> 00:08:02,912
four.

230
00:08:03,092 --> 00:08:05,905
Nobody on Elm Street thought much about

231
00:08:05,905 --> 00:08:06,122
it.

232
00:08:06,302 --> 00:08:08,078
Danny was sixteen.

233
00:08:08,258 --> 00:08:10,453
He was going to be a mechanic.

234
00:08:10,803 --> 00:08:13,265
Every Saturday morning I had my hair

235
00:08:13,265 --> 00:08:15,522
done at the beauty parlor on Park

236
00:08:15,522 --> 00:08:17,779
Avenue, a dollar fifty for a wash

237
00:08:17,779 --> 00:08:18,326
and set.

238
00:08:18,506 --> 00:08:21,094
The beehive was the style that year,

239
00:08:21,094 --> 00:08:23,251
piled up high with a whole can

240
00:08:23,251 --> 00:08:24,113
of Aqua Net.

241
00:08:24,293 --> 00:08:26,279
You slept on a satin pillow and

242
00:08:26,279 --> 00:08:28,264
you didn't touch it for a week.

243
00:08:28,444 --> 00:08:29,775
And Danny, honey,

244
00:08:29,775 --> 00:08:32,438
Danny spent every dollar he had on

245
00:08:32,438 --> 00:08:35,805
a nineteen fifty-two Chevy that didn't run.

246
00:08:35,985 --> 00:08:38,786
He spent the whole summer under it,

247
00:08:38,786 --> 00:08:40,706
in my father's driveway,

248
00:08:40,706 --> 00:08:42,707
singing Beach Boys songs.

249
00:08:42,887 --> 00:08:44,396
Little Deuce Coupe.

250
00:08:44,576 --> 00:08:46,004
Over and over.

251
00:08:46,355 --> 00:08:48,895
In the evenings we listened to the

252
00:08:48,895 --> 00:08:51,659
radio, because we still didn't have a

253
00:08:51,659 --> 00:08:52,705
TV of our own.

254
00:08:52,885 --> 00:08:56,108
Your grandfather would read the sports page

255
00:08:56,108 --> 00:08:57,007
and I'd sew.

256
00:08:57,187 --> 00:08:59,892
I made almost all my own clothes.

257
00:09:00,072 --> 00:09:03,499
A Simplicity pattern cost thirty-five cents,

258
00:09:03,499 --> 00:09:05,836
and a yard of cotton was about

259
00:09:05,836 --> 00:09:06,771
fifty cents.

260
00:09:06,951 --> 00:09:09,149
I made a blue dress that summer

261
00:09:09,149 --> 00:09:11,419
that I wore to every wedding for

262
00:09:11,419 --> 00:09:12,199
five years.

263
00:09:12,549 --> 00:09:14,850
On Sundays we went to dinner at

264
00:09:14,850 --> 00:09:15,741
my mother's.

265
00:09:15,921 --> 00:09:18,365
Pot roast, mashed potatoes,

266
00:09:18,365 --> 00:09:21,353
green beans from the garden and a

267
00:09:21,353 --> 00:09:21,715
pie.

268
00:09:21,895 --> 00:09:24,054
Every single Sunday.

269
00:09:24,234 --> 00:09:25,561
My father carved,

270
00:09:25,561 --> 00:09:26,887
my mother served,

271
00:09:26,887 --> 00:09:28,994
and Danny ate like a horse.

272
00:09:29,174 --> 00:09:31,250
There was a new empty chair at

273
00:09:31,250 --> 00:09:32,704
that table that year.

274
00:09:32,884 --> 00:09:34,784
My grandmother, Pearl,

275
00:09:34,784 --> 00:09:37,806
had passed away the October before.

276
00:09:37,986 --> 00:09:40,111
Nobody sat in her chair.

277
00:09:40,291 --> 00:09:42,056
Not for a long time.

278
00:09:42,406 --> 00:09:43,943
And in September,

279
00:09:43,943 --> 00:09:47,560
sweetheart, I found out I was expecting.

280
00:09:47,740 --> 00:09:48,994
Your Aunt Susan.

281
00:09:49,174 --> 00:09:51,801
I told your grandfather in the car,

282
00:09:51,801 --> 00:09:53,827
in the parking lot of the A

283
00:09:53,827 --> 00:09:56,529
and P, because I couldn't wait until

284
00:09:56,529 --> 00:09:57,429
we got home.

285
00:09:57,609 --> 00:09:59,704
He was so happy he drove into

286
00:09:59,704 --> 00:10:01,510
the shopping cart corral.

287
00:10:01,690 --> 00:10:03,815
We had to pay for two carts.

288
00:10:03,995 --> 00:10:05,423
He didn't care.

289
00:10:05,773 --> 00:10:07,804
That fall was the election.

290
00:10:07,984 --> 00:10:11,711
Johnson against Senator Goldwater from Arizona.

291
00:10:11,891 --> 00:10:14,027
The commercials were frightening.

292
00:10:14,207 --> 00:10:16,474
There was one with a little girl

293
00:10:16,474 --> 00:10:18,387
picking petals off a daisy,

294
00:10:18,387 --> 00:10:19,874
and then a countdown,

295
00:10:19,874 --> 00:10:21,220
and an atomic bomb.

296
00:10:21,400 --> 00:10:23,101
They only showed it once,

297
00:10:23,101 --> 00:10:25,823
but everybody talked about it for weeks.

298
00:10:26,003 --> 00:10:28,511
Johnson won by a landslide.

299
00:10:28,691 --> 00:10:30,119
Forty-four states.

300
00:10:30,469 --> 00:10:32,907
And the Surgeon General had said in

301
00:10:32,907 --> 00:10:35,554
January that cigarettes caused cancer.

302
00:10:35,734 --> 00:10:37,782
It was on the front page of

303
00:10:37,782 --> 00:10:38,996
every newspaper.

304
00:10:39,176 --> 00:10:40,442
And you know what?

305
00:10:40,622 --> 00:10:42,526
Everybody kept smoking.

306
00:10:42,706 --> 00:10:46,275
Your grandfather smoked Pall Malls until nineteen

307
00:10:46,275 --> 00:10:47,222
seventy-five.

308
00:10:47,402 --> 00:10:49,070
In nineteen sixty-four,

309
00:10:49,070 --> 00:10:51,607
honey, you could still smoke in the

310
00:10:51,607 --> 00:10:54,580
hospital waiting room while your wife had

311
00:10:54,580 --> 00:10:55,088
a baby.

312
00:10:55,438 --> 00:10:56,989
Christmas that year,

313
00:10:56,989 --> 00:10:59,315
your grandfather surprised me.

314
00:10:59,495 --> 00:11:02,411
He'd been working double shifts in secret

315
00:11:02,411 --> 00:11:03,478
for two months.

316
00:11:03,658 --> 00:11:06,937
On Christmas morning there was a television

317
00:11:06,937 --> 00:11:09,835
in the corner of our little apartment,

318
00:11:09,835 --> 00:11:11,818
a Zenith, black and white,

319
00:11:11,818 --> 00:11:13,038
nineteen inches.

320
00:11:13,218 --> 00:11:14,774
Two hundred dollars.

321
00:11:14,954 --> 00:11:17,137
Bonanza on Sunday nights.

322
00:11:17,317 --> 00:11:19,557
I think I cried more over that

323
00:11:19,557 --> 00:11:22,321
television than I did at our wedding.

324
00:11:22,671 --> 00:11:25,150
So that was nineteen sixty-four,

325
00:11:25,150 --> 00:11:26,003
sweetheart.

326
00:11:26,183 --> 00:11:27,715
It wasn't perfect.

327
00:11:27,895 --> 00:11:29,300
Nothing ever is.

328
00:11:29,480 --> 00:11:31,630
There was a war starting that would

329
00:11:31,630 --> 00:11:33,104
break my family's heart,

330
00:11:33,104 --> 00:11:35,622
and there were people fighting for rights

331
00:11:35,622 --> 00:11:37,526
they should have had all along.

332
00:11:37,706 --> 00:11:39,970
But we didn't know what was coming.

333
00:11:40,150 --> 00:11:41,171
We were young,

334
00:11:41,171 --> 00:11:42,337
we were in love,

335
00:11:42,337 --> 00:11:44,817
and the Beatles were on the radio.

336
00:11:45,167 --> 00:11:47,256
What I miss most is how little

337
00:11:47,256 --> 00:11:47,953
we needed.

338
00:11:48,133 --> 00:11:49,828
Two rooms, one car,

339
00:11:49,828 --> 00:11:52,682
fifteen dollars a week for food,

340
00:11:52,682 --> 00:11:53,752
and a radio.

341
00:11:53,932 --> 00:11:55,836
And we thought we were rich.

342
00:11:56,016 --> 00:11:57,836
Because we had each other,

343
00:11:57,836 --> 00:11:59,096
and Sunday dinner,

344
00:11:59,096 --> 00:12:01,405
and a whole future out there that

345
00:12:01,405 --> 00:12:03,435
looked like the World's Fair.

346
00:12:03,615 --> 00:12:04,753
That feeling.

347
00:12:04,933 --> 00:12:06,907
That's what I'd go back for.

348
00:12:07,257 --> 00:12:09,387
Now, what year would you go back

349
00:12:09,387 --> 00:12:10,519
to, if you could?

350
00:12:10,699 --> 00:12:13,253
And if your grandparents were young in

351
00:12:13,253 --> 00:12:15,941
sixty-four, ask them where they were the

352
00:12:15,941 --> 00:12:17,688
night the Beatles came on.

353
00:12:17,868 --> 00:12:19,575
Tell me in the comments.

354
00:12:19,755 --> 00:12:21,789
And come back tomorrow,

355
00:12:21,789 --> 00:12:22,762
sweetheart.

356
00:12:22,942 --> 00:12:25,659
Grandma has a lot more stories.

357
00:12:25,839 --> 00:12:26,791
Goodnight.
